-- THE ARMING as a VALUE (EVERY-EACH-QUANTIFIER-SPEC §2.6 card B1) is the
-- instant an obligation was entered. The machine binds it in a deonton's
-- own slots as it arms the obligation, on an EVERY's join line, and at
-- every HENCE or LEST hand-off; nowhere else. So the checker refuses it
-- everywhere else (refusal 8 of §5.1.1.1, card B1c, RULED 2026-10-24):
-- written in one of the places below it used to check clean and be
-- refused by the run on every input.
--
-- Moved here from ok/every/run-lifecycle-values.l4 §11, which pinned the
-- run-time refusal of the first case.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Tenant HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign HAS signer IS AN Actor

alice MEANS Tenant OF "Alice"

-- refused: `t` is a NUMBER the rule defines in a top-level WHERE, outside
-- any obligation; no arming has happened where it is written
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`arming defined outside` MEANS
    PARTY alice MUST Sign alice WITHIN 5 OF t
    WHERE t MEANS THE ARMING

-- refused: a function's body is read where the function is defined, not
-- where it is called, so calling it from a WITHIN does not help
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`d after the arming` MEANS THE ARMING PLUS d

-- refused: a top-level rule's body
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the arming` MEANS THE ARMING

-- not refused: in the obligation's own WITHIN it is that obligation's
-- arming, and a function receives it as an argument from there
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`arming passed in` MEANS
    PARTY alice MUST Sign alice WITHIN 0 OF (`plus five` THE ARMING)

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`plus five` MEANS x PLUS 5
